snp3.5:22.1 | S: “Clearly my questions were not in vain!” | T: Māgha: “Yes, my question was not in vain.
snp3.5:22.2 | S: said Māgha, | T: (none)
snp3.5:22.3 | S: “The Buddha has explained to me <j>who is worthy of donations. | T: You, Blessed One, have pointed out the recipient.
snp3.5:22.4 | S: You are the one here who knows this to be true, | T: You know this here, as it really is,
snp3.5:22.5 | S: for truly you understand this matter. | T: because this Dhamma has been experienced by you.
snp3.5:23.1 | S: Suppose a lay donor who is committed to charity | T: When a householder responsive to requests, a master of giving,
snp3.5:23.2 | S: makes a sacrifice seeking merit, looking for merit, | T: makes a sacrifice in hopes of merit, looking for merit,
snp3.5:23.3 | S: giving food and drink to others here: | T: giving food & drink to others here:
snp3.5:23.4 | S: explain to me how to accomplish the sacrifice.” | T: Point out to me, Blessed One, the consummation of the sacrifice.”
snp3.5:24.1 | S: “Sacrifice, and while doing so,” | T: The Buddha: “Sacrifice, and while sacrificing,
snp3.5:24.2 | S: replied the Buddha, | T: Māgha, said the Blessed One,
snp3.5:24.3 | S: “be clear and confident in every way. | T: make your mind clear everywhere.
snp3.5:24.4 | S: Sacrifice is the ground standing upon which | T: For one sacrificing, the sacrifice is the object, the support. Taking a stand there,
snp3.5:24.5 | S: the sacrificer sheds their flaws. | T: he abandons faults.
snp3.5:25.1 | S: One free of greed, rid of anger, | T: He—devoid of passion, subduing aversion,
snp3.5:25.2 | S: developing a heart of limitless love, | T: developing a mind of goodwill immeasurable,
snp3.5:25.3 | S: spreads that limitlessness in every direction, | T: day & night, continually heedful— should pervade all the directions immeasurably.”
snp3.5:25.4 | S: ever diligent day and night.” | T: (none)
snp3.5:26.1 | S: “Who is purified, freed, awake? | T: Māgha: “Who is purified? Released? Bound?
snp3.5:26.2 | S: How can one go to the realm of divinity oneself? | T: By means of what mind does one go to the Brahmā world?
snp3.5:26.3 | S: I do not know, so please tell me when asked, | T: Say this, sage, when asked by me who doesn’t know.
snp3.5:26.4 | S: for the Buddha is the divinity I see in person today! | T: As the Blessed One is my witness, I have seen Brahmā today,
snp3.5:26.5 | S: To us you are truly the equal of divinity. | T: for you are equal to Brahmā for us—it’s true!
snp3.5:26.6 | S: Brilliant One, how is one reborn <j>in the realm of divinity?” | T: How, shining one, does one reappear in the Brahmā world?”
snp3.5:27.1 | S: “One who accomplishes the sacrifice <j>with three modes,” | T: The Buddha: “Whoever sacrifices with the threefold sacrifice consummation,
snp3.5:27.2 | S: replied the Buddha, | T: (none)
snp3.5:27.3 | S: “such a one would succeed <j>due to those who are worthy of donations. | T: such a one would achieve his aim in terms of the recipient.
snp3.5:27.4 | S: Sacrificing like this, one rightly committed to charity | T: Having thus sacrificed rightly, one responsive to requests
snp3.5:27.5 | S: is reborn in the realm of divinity, I say.” | T: reappears, I say, in the Brahmā world.”
snp3.5:28.1 | S: When he had spoken, the student Māgha said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, the young brahman Māgha said to the Blessed One:
snp3.5:28.2 | S: “Excellent, worthy Gotama! Excellent! … | T: “Magnificent, Master Gotama! Magnificent! Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has Master Gotama—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear. I go to Master Gotama for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks.
snp3.5:28.3 | S: From this day forth, may the worthy Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: May Master Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge from this day forward, for life.”
snp3.5:28.4 | P: Māghasuttaṁ pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.72
an10.72:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Vesālī, at the Great Wood, in the hall with the peaked roof, together with several well-known senior disciples. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Vesālī at the Gabled Hall in the Great Forest together with many very well-known elder disciples:
an10.72:1.2 | S: They included the venerables Cāla, Upacāla, Kakkaṭa, Kaṭimbha, Kaṭa, Kaṭissaṅga, | T: Ven. Pāla, Ven. Upapāla, Ven. Kakkaṭa, Ven. Kaḷimbha, Ven. Nikaṭa, Ven. Kaṭissaha,
an10.72:1.3 | S: and other well-known senior disciples. | T: & other very well-known elder disciples.
an10.72:2.1 | S: Now at that time several well-known Licchavis plunged deep into the Great Wood to see the Buddha. Driving a succession of fine carriages, they made a colossal racket. | T: And on that occasion many very well-known Licchavis—racing after one another in auspicious vehicles, making a shrill noise, a great noise—plunged into the Great Forest to see the Blessed One.
an10.72:2.2 | S: Then those venerables thought: | T: Then the thought occurred to the venerable ones:
an10.72:2.3 | S: “These several well-known Licchavis have plunged deep into the Great Wood to see the Buddha. Driving a succession of fine carriages, they’re making a colossal racket. | T: “These many very well-known Licchavis—racing after one another in auspicious vehicles, making a shrill noise, a great noise—are plunging into the Great Forest to see the Blessed One.
an10.72:2.4 | S: But the Buddha has said that sound is a thorn to absorption. | T: Now, the jhānas are said by the Blessed One to be thorned by noise.
an10.72:2.5 | S: Let’s go to the Gosiṅga Sal Wood. | T: What if we were to go to the Gosiṅga Sāla forest park?
an10.72:2.6 | S: There we can meditate comfortably, free of noise and crowds.” | T: There we would live comfortably, with next-to-no noise, next-to-no crowding.”
an10.72:2.7 | S: Then those venerables went to the Gosiṅga Sal Wood, | T: So the venerable ones went to Gosiṅga Sāla forest park.
an10.72:2.8 | S: where they meditated comfortably, free of noise and crowds. | T: There they lived comfortably, with next-to-no noise, next-to-no crowding.
an10.72:3.1 | S: Then the Buddha said to the mendicants: | T: Then the Blessed One addressed the monks:
an10.72:3.2 | S: “Mendicants, where are Cāla, Upacāla, Kakkaṭa, Kaṭimbha, Kaṭa, and Kaṭissaṅga? | T: “Where is Pāla, monks? Where is Upapāla? Where is Kakkaṭa? Where is Kaḷimbha? Where is Nikaṭa? Where is Kaṭissaha?
an10.72:3.3 | S: Where have these senior disciples gone?” | T: Where have those elder disciples gone?”
an10.72:4.1 | S: And the mendicants told him what had happened. | T: “Just now, lord, the thought occurred to those venerable ones,
an10.72:4.2 | P: ‘ime kho sambahulā abhiññātā abhiññātā licchavī bhadrehi bhadrehi yānehi parapurāya uccāsaddā mahāsaddā mahāvanaṁ ajjhogāhanti bhagavantaṁ dassanāya saddakaṇṭakā kho pana jhānā vuttā bhagavatā yannūna mayaṁ yena gosiṅgasālavanadāyo tenupasaṅkameyyāma tattha mayaṁ appasaddā appākiṇṇā phāsuṁ vihareyyāmā’ti. | T: ‘These many very well-known Licchavis—racing after one another in auspicious vehicles, making a shrill noise, a great noise—are plunging into the Great Forest to see the Blessed One. Now, the jhānas are said by the Blessed One to be thorned by noise. What if we were to go to the Gosiṅga Sāla forest park? There we would live comfortably, with next-to-no noise, next-to-no crowding.’
an10.72:4.3 | P: Atha kho te, bhante, āyasmanto yena gosiṅgasālavanadāyo tenupasaṅkamiṁsu. | T: So those venerable ones went to Gosiṅga Sāla forest park.
an10.72:4.4 | P: Tattha te āyasmanto appasaddā appākiṇṇā phāsuṁ viharantī”ti. | T: There they are living comfortably, with next-to-no noise, next-to-no crowding.”
an10.72:5.1 | S: “Good, good, mendicants! It’s just as those great disciples have so rightly explained. I have said that sound is a thorn to absorption. | T: “Very good, monks, very good—what those great disciples, rightly declaring, have declared, for the jhānas have been said by me to be thorned by noise.
an10.72:6.1 | S: Mendicants, there are these ten thorns. | T: “Monks, there are these ten thorns.
an10.72:6.2 | S: What ten? | T: Which ten?
an10.72:6.3 | S: Relishing company is a thorn for someone who loves seclusion. Focusing on the feature of beauty is a thorn for someone pursuing the meditation on the feature of ugliness. Seeing shows is a thorn to someone restraining the senses. Lingering in the neighborhood of females is a thorn to chastity. Sound is a thorn to the first absorption. Placing the mind and keeping it connected are a thorn to the second absorption. Rapture is a thorn to the third absorption. Breathing is a thorn to the fourth absorption. Perception and feeling are a thorn to the attainment of the cessation of perception and feeling. Greed, hate, and delusion are thorns. | T: “For one who loves seclusion, love of entanglement is a thorn. “For one committed to the theme of the unattractive, commitment to the theme of the attractive is a thorn. “For one guarding the sense doors, watching a show is a thorn. “For one practicing celibacy, nearness to women is a thorn. “For the first jhāna, noise is a thorn. “For the second jhāna, directed thoughts & evaluations are thorns. “For the third jhāna, rapture is a thorn. “For the fourth jhāna, in-and-out breaths are thorns. “For the attainment of the cessation of perception & feeling, perception & feelings are thorns. “Passion is a thorn. Aversion is a thorn. Delusion is a thorn.
an10.72:7.1 | S: Mendicants, live free of thorns! | T: “Dwell unthorned, monks!
an10.72:7.2 | S: Live rid of thorns! | T: Dwell unthorned & dethorned!
an10.72:7.3 | S: Mendicants, live free of thorns and rid of thorns! | T: (none)
an10.72:7.4 | S: The perfected ones live free of thorns, | T: The arahants are unthorned, monks.
an10.72:7.5 | S: rid of thorns, | T: (none)
an10.72:7.6 | S: free and rid of thorns.” | T: The arahants are unthorned & dethorned.”
an10.72:7.7 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.173
an4.173:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita went up to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Mahā Koṭṭhita went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an4.173:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, Mahākoṭṭhita sat down to one side, and said to Sāriputta: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Sāriputta,
an4.173:2.1 | S: “Reverend, when the six fields of contact have faded away and ceased with no residue left behind, does something else still exist?” | T: “With the remainderless fading & cessation of the six contact-media [vision, hearing, smell, taste, touch, & intellection], is it the case that there is anything else?”
an4.173:3.1 | S: “Don’t put it like that, reverend.” | T: [Ven. Sāriputta:] “Don’t say that, my friend.”
an4.173:4.1 | S: “Does something else no longer exist?” | T: [Ven. Mahā Koṭṭhita:] “With the remainderless fading & cessation of the six contact-media, is it the case that there is not anything else?”
an4.173:5.1 | S: “Don’t put it like that, reverend.” | T: [Ven. Sāriputta:] “Don’t say that, my friend.”
an4.173:6.1 | S: “Does something else both still exist and no longer exist?” | T: [Ven. Mahā Koṭṭhita:] “…is it the case that there both is & is not anything else?”
an4.173:7.1 | S: “Don’t put it like that, reverend.” | T: [Ven. Sāriputta:] “Don’t say that, my friend.”
an4.173:8.1 | S: “Does something else neither still exist nor no longer exist?” | T: [Ven. Mahā Koṭṭhita:] “…is it the case that there neither is nor is not anything else?”
an4.173:9.1 | S: “Don’t put it like that, reverend.” | T: [Ven. Sāriputta:] “Don’t say that, my friend.”
an4.173:10.1 | S: “Reverend, when asked whether—when the six fields of contact have faded away and ceased with no residue left behind—something else still exists, you say ‘don’t put it like that’. | T: [Ven. Mahā Koṭṭhita:] “Being asked if, with the remainderless fading & cessation of the six contact-media, there is anything else, you say, ‘Don’t say that, my friend.’
an4.173:10.2 | S: When asked whether something else no longer exists, | T: Being asked if… there is not anything else…
an4.173:10.3 | S: you say ‘don’t put it like that’. | T: (none)
an4.173:10.4 | S: When asked whether something else both still exists and no longer exists, | T: there both is & is not anything else…
an4.173:10.5 | S: you say ‘don’t put it like that’. | T: (none)
an4.173:10.6 | S: When asked whether something else neither still exists nor no longer exists, | T: there neither is nor is not anything else,
an4.173:10.7 | S: you say ‘don’t put it like that’. | T: you say, ‘Don’t say that, my friend.’
an4.173:10.8 | S: How then should we see the meaning of this statement?” | T: Now, how is the meaning of your words to be understood?”
an4.173:11.1 | S: “In saying that, ‘When the six fields of contact have faded away and ceased with no residue left behind, something else still exists’, you’re proliferating the unproliferated. | T: [Ven. Sāriputta:] “The statement, ‘With the remainderless fading & cessation of the six contact-media, is it the case that there is anything else?’ objectifies the unobjectified.
an4.173:11.2 | S: In saying that, ‘something else no longer exists’, you’re proliferating the unproliferated. | T: The statement, ‘… is it the case that there is not anything else…
an4.173:11.3 | S: In saying that, ‘something else both still exists and no longer exists’, you’re proliferating the unproliferated. | T: is it the case that there both is & is not anything else…
an4.173:11.4 | S: In saying that, ‘something else neither still exists nor no longer exists’, you’re proliferating the unproliferated. | T: is it the case that there neither is nor is not anything else?’ objectifies the unobjectified.
an4.173:11.5 | S: The scope of proliferation extends as far as the scope of the six fields of contact. | T: However far the six contact-media go, that is how far objectification goes.
an4.173:11.6 | S: The scope of the six fields of contact extends as far as the scope of proliferation. | T: However far objectification goes, that is how far the six contact-media go.
an4.173:11.7 | S: When the six fields of contact fade away and cease with no residue left behind, proliferation stops and is stilled.” | T: With the remainderless fading & cessation of the six contact-media, there comes to be the cessation of objectification, the stilling of objectification.”
an4.173:11.8 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

